I agree on your last statement but disagree with your conclusion. What
my impression of Ellora's story (Krysia's experience) was is that it
had been based on several bases of knowledge; NH Guidebook, D&D, Real
Life, are the three I still can remember. The latter had helped her a
lot in her progress. I also agree on the "indifferently used realism"
in Nethack, but it's still a/the main source of explorative gameplay.
Any realism introduced will add to the consistency of the human
"default system" (as I called it above). The consistencies of the
other systems in Nethack (like "the" magic system) may conflict with
realism, maybe as a design choice, or maybe inadvertently. Introducing
realism _for any price_ is a Bad Thing, but generally I am in favour
of it, as long as it does not conflict with gameplay. And talking
about more realistic features is fine, until they are dismissed for
one or another reason.

